// Mini-notation
// Inside the quotes is a small
// language of its own.
// x*n plays x n times in its step.
// x/n plays it once every n cycles.
//
// Try: change hh*8 into hh*16 or
// hh*6, and cp/2 into cp/4.

setcpm(30)
$: s("bd*2 sd")
$: s("hh*8").gain(0.5)
$: s("~ cp/2")
// ---
// Brackets
// [ ] squeezes several steps into
// one: in "bd [sd sd]" the second
// half is split in two again.
//
// Try: write rhythms of your own,
// with brackets inside brackets.

setcpm(30)
$: s("bd [~ bd] sd [~ sd]")
$: s("hh*8").gain(0.5)
// ---
// Angle brackets
// < > plays one of its items per
// cycle, in turn: "<c e g>" plays c,
// then e, then g, then c again.
//
// Try: add a note to the bass. Put
// <sd cp> in place of a snare.

setcpm(30)
$: s("bd sd bd sd")
$: note("<c2 eb2 f2 g2>")
  .s("sawtooth").lpf(600)
// ---
// Commas
// A comma plays things at the same
// time: "bd*2, hh*4" is two rhythms
// in one string. Between notes, a
// comma makes a chord: [c3,e3,g3]
//
// Try: change the notes of the
// chords, or add a fourth to each.

setcpm(30)
$: s("bd*2 sd, hh*4")
$: note("<[c3,e3,g3] [a2,c3,e3]>")
  .s("piano")
// ---
// Repeat, stretch, maybe
// x!3 repeats a step: "bd!3 sd" is
// "bd bd bd sd".
// x@3 makes a step 3 times longer.
// x? plays only half of the time.
//
// Try: change @3 into @2, and add ?
// to more steps.

setcpm(30)
$: s("bd!3 sd")
$: note("c3@3 eb3 g3@2 f3")
  .s("triangle")
$: s("hh*8?").gain(0.5)
// ---
// Euclidean rhythms
// bd(3,8) spreads 3 hits as evenly
// as it can over 8 steps: rhythms
// heard in music all over the world.
//
// Try: (5,8), (3,7), (7,16). A third
// number turns the rhythm: (3,8,2)

setcpm(30)
$: s("bd(3,8)")
$: s("hh(5,8)").gain(0.5)
$: s("~ sd")
